WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:08.100
My friends, Argentina isn’t having its best
moment.

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:19.449
Mauricio Macri’s government has had to ask
for a bailout from the International Monetary

00:00:19.449 --> 00:00:24.520
Fund, and the truth is that, in the last few
decades, crises, devaluations, inflation and

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:29.050
payment suspensions have been commonplace
in Argentina's politics and economy.

00:00:29.050 --> 00:00:32.840
Well, throughout this entire process… one
political movement has had almost absolute

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:41.820
influence: Peronism.

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:50.800
But, ok…

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:54.300
What exactly is this political stream known
as Peronism?

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:55.460
How did it come about?

00:00:55.460 --> 00:00:57.680
What impact has it really had and how has
it influenced Argentina?

00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:01.329
My friends, we’ll see it here on VisualPolitik
in two videos.

00:01:01.329 --> 00:01:05.509
And in the first one, we’ll focus on its
origins and identity.

00:01:05.509 --> 00:01:10.960
Listen up.

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:17.869
Who was Juan Domingo Perón?

00:01:17.869 --> 00:01:22.709
And, most importantly, how did he manage to
gain power and influence Argentine politics

00:01:22.709 --> 00:01:24.079
for the following 7 decades?

00:01:24.079 --> 00:01:27.039
Dear viewers, to begin this story, we have
to go back to the 30s.

00:01:27.039 --> 00:01:38.899
At that time, Argentine nationalism was in
its golden age.

00:01:38.899 --> 00:01:43.709
Argentina was, undoubtedly, Latin America’s
great power: despite the 1929 crisis it was

00:01:43.709 --> 00:01:49.499
still one of the 10 richest countries in the
world and wages were much, much higher than

00:01:49.499 --> 00:01:51.700
in old Europe.

00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:52.799
However...

00:01:52.799 --> 00:02:02.229
just a moment, because in spite of this, the
29 crisis had taken its toll, hitting the

00:02:02.229 --> 00:02:03.229
country hard...

00:02:03.229 --> 00:02:07.950
This tension led to the first military coup
in 1930, a coup supported by many political,

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:17.700
economic and social sectors, especially the
conservative side.

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:22.689
And under this militaristic, authoritarian
and nationalist panorama, it became customary

00:02:22.689 --> 00:02:28.099
for many officers of the Argentine army to
be sent to Germany and Italy to take advanced

00:02:28.099 --> 00:02:29.659
courses.

00:02:29.659 --> 00:02:37.260
And, my friends, do you know who one of these
officers was?

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:42.480
Exactly, Juan Domingo Perón, who was sent
to Italy, a country where he met and studied

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:45.620
first-hand how Benito Mussolini’s government
was organized.

00:02:45.620 --> 00:02:46.830
And, you know what?

00:02:46.830 --> 00:02:52.460
Soon after, he’d get the chance to put this
knowledge into practice.

00:02:52.460 --> 00:02:59.740
See, at the beginning of the 1940s, Argentina
was under the control of a corrupt and authoritarian

00:02:59.740 --> 00:03:04.609
government, a government that had perpetrated
electoral fraud and attempted to stay in power

00:03:04.609 --> 00:03:09.569
at all costs.

00:03:09.569 --> 00:03:14.650
Under these circumstances, on June 4th, 1943,
a group of young officers in the Argentine

00:03:14.650 --> 00:03:20.370
army, held a coup d'etat, supposedly to restore
a transparent electoral system.

00:03:20.370 --> 00:03:24.680
One of these officers was, precisely, our
protagonist, the Lieutenant Colonel, Juan

00:03:24.680 --> 00:03:26.280
Domingo Perón.

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:33.799
By the way, this was Argentina’s second
coup d'état after the 1930’s one… but

00:03:33.799 --> 00:03:37.209
don’t you think it was its last, not at
all.

00:03:37.209 --> 00:03:39.409
During the next 30 years the country suffered...

00:03:39.409 --> 00:03:42.150
4 more coups!!!

00:03:42.150 --> 00:03:47.739
The case is, dear VisualPolitik viewers, that
it was this 1943 coup... which opened the

00:03:47.739 --> 00:03:51.840
doors to politics and power for Lieutenant
Colonel Juan Domingo Perón.

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:56.349
We can practically say that this is where
Argentina’s modern history began.

00:03:56.349 --> 00:03:57.349
Listen up

00:03:57.349 --> 00:04:11.959
(THE MASTER PLAY)

00:04:11.959 --> 00:04:16.569
During the two and a half years that the military
government lasted, Perón's career was meteoric...

00:04:16.569 --> 00:04:19.829
Even though he’d held a very low rank until
then.

00:04:19.829 --> 00:04:23.940
Positions such as Head of the Department of
Labor, a position that in principle was, how

00:04:23.940 --> 00:04:25.600
do I say it, not very shiny, very bureaucratic.

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:29.569
However, just a moment, because thanks to
his political intelligence, Perón managed

00:04:29.569 --> 00:04:33.130
to use this position as a springboard for
his rise to power.

00:04:33.130 --> 00:04:36.940
See, from the very beginning, Juan Domingo
Perón used everything he’d learned under

00:04:36.940 --> 00:04:44.370
Benito Mussolini in Italy, starting by winning
some of the unions’ support.

00:04:44.370 --> 00:04:48.699
That way, each time the Labor Department had
to intervene in a conflict between employers

00:04:48.699 --> 00:04:54.229
and unions related to Perón... the former
(the employers) would almost always lose.

00:04:54.229 --> 00:04:59.319
As you can imagine, this meant that this military
man’s popularity didn’t stop growing.

00:04:59.319 --> 00:05:05.760
In fact, shortly after, Perón was appointed
Minister of War and then...

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:06.820
Vice President...

00:05:06.820 --> 00:05:10.300
All without forgetting the labor policy and
the unions...

00:05:10.300 --> 00:05:13.990
Because if something was very, very clear
to this Lieutenant Colonel, it was that that

00:05:13.990 --> 00:05:17.710
would be his political springboard.

00:05:17.710 --> 00:05:26.949
But...he wasn’t the only one aspiring to
power... and so much prominence led to a lot

00:05:26.949 --> 00:05:31.650
of jealousy… so much that the military leaders
asked him to move away from the government

00:05:31.650 --> 00:05:33.000
in 1945...

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:38.600
So we could expect this to be the end of this
military man’s political career....

00:05:38.600 --> 00:05:39.600
Right?

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:41.360
Nothing could be further from the truth.

00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:46.539
Perón, had learned how much influence the
media could have in Europe...

00:05:46.539 --> 00:05:52.560
so he asked his superiors if he could say
goodbye through the national radio network.

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:56.370
They naively accepted and Perón took full
advantage of the opportunity.

00:05:56.370 --> 00:06:00.449
In his "farewell" message, he thanked the
working classes for their support and questioned

00:06:00.449 --> 00:06:14.080
the continuity of the social benefits he’d
promoted.

00:06:14.080 --> 00:06:38.400
It had a 

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:39.400
huge effect…

00:06:39.400 --> 00:06:43.720
the following days, the unions mobilized in
his favor... and on October 17th thousands

00:06:43.720 --> 00:06:51.790
of workers occupied the Plaza de Mayo, the
nerve center of Argentine politics.

00:06:51.790 --> 00:07:03.900
The government, disoriented by the way events
unfolded, asked the Lieutenant Colonel himself

00:07:03.900 --> 00:07:05.039
to appease the masses,

00:07:05.039 --> 00:07:09.310
Which he did… but only after agreeing that
he’d be a candidate in the next elections

00:07:09.310 --> 00:07:12.240
with the army leaders, and that he’d have,
let’s say, the privileged support of the

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:13.939
public powers.

00:07:13.939 --> 00:07:16.580
Listen up.

00:07:16.580 --> 00:07:23.180
(AN ASSAULT TO THE HEAVENS)

00:07:23.180 --> 00:07:26.470
Juan Domingo Perón had managed to become
a candidate...

00:07:26.470 --> 00:07:29.949
now he needed to win the elections.

00:07:29.949 --> 00:07:34.990
As we’ve seen, at that time, Argentina was
under military control, so government authorization

00:07:34.990 --> 00:07:42.409
was required to hold meetings... and electoral
acts were no exception.

00:07:42.409 --> 00:07:46.940
Of course, Perón's campaign actions were
almost automatically authorized...

00:07:46.940 --> 00:07:50.080
while his opponents’… well let's say the
process was much slower.

00:07:50.080 --> 00:07:53.740
And not only that, Perón also had almost
complete access to the most important means

00:07:53.740 --> 00:07:59.300
of communication at the time: the national
radio station, which was owned by the government.

00:07:59.300 --> 00:08:11.039
But that's not all, under Peron’s insistence,
the government approved paid vacations for

00:08:11.039 --> 00:08:14.730
all workers, as well as an additional salary
at the end of the year.

00:08:14.730 --> 00:08:17.510
Don’t tell me those aren’t good electoral
claims.

00:08:17.510 --> 00:08:22.789
And there was still a move this genius of
political strategies managed to use very well.

00:08:22.789 --> 00:08:28.650
See, the State Department of the United States
itself intervened in Peron’s rivals’ campaigns,

00:08:28.650 --> 00:08:32.630
publishing a report known as the "blue book"
where, Peron’s activities, and those of

00:08:32.630 --> 00:08:36.640
his military allies, were shown to support
the Nazi regime.

00:08:36.640 --> 00:08:43.330
However, my friends, Perón managed to turn
this around and morph it into something like

00:08:43.330 --> 00:08:47.880
a struggle between himself, who represented
Argentine sovereignty, and the United States.

00:08:47.880 --> 00:08:52.450
In the end, all these tactics, copied years
later by populist governments throughout Latin

00:08:52.450 --> 00:08:54.880
America, won the elections for Peron.

00:08:54.880 --> 00:08:59.590
The victory wasn’t overwhelming, but he
won and that was enough.

00:08:59.590 --> 00:09:06.550
(THE PERONIST GOVERNMENT)

00:09:06.550 --> 00:09:13.310
A very important fact: when Juan Domingo Perón
came to the government, Argentina was one

00:09:13.310 --> 00:09:17.090
of the 10 most prosperous and richest economies
in the world.

00:09:17.090 --> 00:09:19.970
The country was even an international creditor.

00:09:19.970 --> 00:09:22.810
Countries like the United Kingdom owed it
money.

00:09:22.810 --> 00:09:27.260
Thanks to all this, the new president had
more than enough resources to launch his political

00:09:27.260 --> 00:09:30.410
program, a program based on 3 pillars:

00:09:30.410 --> 00:09:33.510
Political sovereignty, economic independence
and social justice.

00:09:33.510 --> 00:09:39.380
In practice, he transformed Argentina into
a kind of army under the command of a single

00:09:39.380 --> 00:09:41.500
leader, its president.

00:09:41.500 --> 00:09:47.210
Thus, Perón managed to nationalize a lot
of companies, especially those related to

00:09:47.210 --> 00:09:51.140
public services such as transport, energy
and communications.

00:09:51.140 --> 00:09:55.600
He also placed banking under governmental
control and approved a lot of foreign trade

00:09:55.600 --> 00:09:59.870
restrictions.

00:09:59.870 --> 00:10:03.430
And so, he achieved an important amount of
popular support, how?

00:10:03.430 --> 00:10:08.351
By maintaining utility rates even below their
cost and hiring a lot of employees for the

00:10:08.351 --> 00:10:10.980
government and for governmental companies...

00:10:10.980 --> 00:10:12.950
What a coincidence...

00:10:12.950 --> 00:10:16.090
the Kirchners, 60 years later, did practically
the same

00:10:16.090 --> 00:10:17.150
Of course...

00:10:17.150 --> 00:10:20.700
all that involved a lot of public spending...
and how do you think he financed it?

00:10:20.700 --> 00:10:21.700
1, 2,3...

00:10:21.700 --> 00:10:22.700
Right!

00:10:22.700 --> 00:10:23.700
By running the bill printing machine…

00:10:23.700 --> 00:10:29.740
Yes, now we know where the addiction of Argentine
politicians began for printing bills.

00:10:29.740 --> 00:10:39.850
The fact is that during the first years of
this entire process, and thanks to all the

00:10:39.850 --> 00:10:45.310
money that he injected into the economy, things
seemed to be sailing smoothly...

00:10:45.310 --> 00:10:46.420
The problem?

00:10:46.420 --> 00:10:50.710
It simply wasn’t sustainable... as the government
overspent… the country's competitiveness

00:10:50.710 --> 00:10:52.890
began to sink faster than the Titanic.

00:10:52.890 --> 00:10:57.310
Within a few years, their positive balance
vanished; Private investment collapsed and

00:10:57.310 --> 00:11:00.670
suddenly, some prices started to skyrocket.

00:11:00.670 --> 00:11:04.740
By the way, what do you think happened then?

00:11:04.740 --> 00:11:08.860
Do you think that, given that his "revolution"
didn’t seem to be on the right track, Perón

00:11:08.860 --> 00:11:10.410
took a bit of the blame?

00:11:10.410 --> 00:11:16.540
Nope... not at all... he blamed alleged “speculators”
of attacking the national economy.

00:11:16.540 --> 00:11:20.810
And to remedy it, of course, he multiplied
the government controls, the regulated prices

00:11:20.810 --> 00:11:25.610
and implemented all kinds of measures that
would even affect bread distribution...

00:11:25.610 --> 00:11:26.610
in Argentina!

00:11:26.610 --> 00:11:27.610
In the world’s barn!

00:11:27.610 --> 00:11:29.070
But, my friends, the crisis wasn’t Peron’s
only problem…

00:11:29.070 --> 00:11:33.620
the 1853 Constitution didn’t allow for a
politician to stay in power, preventing an

00:11:33.620 --> 00:11:35.160
immediate re-election.

00:11:35.160 --> 00:11:37.440
So under this scenario, what do you think
he did?

00:11:37.440 --> 00:11:40.930
Listen up.

00:11:40.930 --> 00:11:49.839
(THE POLITICAL REFORM)

00:11:49.839 --> 00:11:53.570
Along with the bill printing machine, Latin
American politicians’ second favorite toy

00:11:53.570 --> 00:11:55.420
is constituent reforms.

00:11:55.420 --> 00:11:57.110
And, of course, Perón was no exception.

00:11:57.110 --> 00:12:04.310
In 1949, since the previous Constitution imposed
too many obstacles...

00:12:04.310 --> 00:12:06.580
Perón launched a complete constitutional
reform.

00:12:06.580 --> 00:12:13.670
The new Constitution placed the state as the
fundamental pillar of Argentine society.

00:12:13.670 --> 00:12:17.810
Even in the Constitution’s own preamble,
the three pillars of Juan Domingo Perón's

00:12:17.810 --> 00:12:19.110
political theses were included.

00:12:19.110 --> 00:12:22.110
This was supposed to be a Constitution for
all...

00:12:22.110 --> 00:12:26.410
But let’s take a look at these changes’
results:

00:12:26.410 --> 00:12:30.180
Investments had disappeared, the macro data
just got worse, the country was in crisis

00:12:30.180 --> 00:12:35.830
and public companies in Argentina underwent
what the PDVSA -Venezuelan state-owned oil

00:12:35.830 --> 00:12:37.990
and natural gas company- underwent…

00:12:37.990 --> 00:12:42.840
they weren’t invested in, so they suffered
from all kinds of influences and in the end...

00:12:42.840 --> 00:12:50.730
they were very unproductive and inefficient...
to the detriment of all Argentines.

00:12:50.730 --> 00:12:51.730
In other words...

00:12:51.730 --> 00:12:56.130
the new model Juan Domingo Perón had implemented
became a poverty-making machine...

00:12:56.130 --> 00:12:59.440
Of course, this all happened under an enormous
cult to the leader...

00:12:59.440 --> 00:13:04.310
The case, my friends, is that the Argentine
society was in crisis and so fragmented that

00:13:04.310 --> 00:13:08.740
under these circumstances in September 1955
there was a new coup d'etat.

00:13:08.740 --> 00:13:23.530
This time against Juan Domingo Perón.

00:13:23.530 --> 00:13:55.090
Now, do you think this was Peron’s end?

00:13:55.090 --> 00:14:01.290
Well, if you do… you are highly mistaken…
but we’ll talk about all of that in a future

00:14:01.290 --> 00:14:02.290
video.

00:14:02.290 --> 00:14:05.810
I really hope you enjoyed this video, please
hit like if you did and don’t forget to

00:14:05.810 --> 00:14:08.020
subscribe to our channel for brand new videos..

00:14:08.020 --> 00:14:12.950
Also, don’t forget to check out our friends
at the Reconsider Media Podcast - they provided

00:14:12.950 --> 00:14:16.010
the vocals in this episode that were not mine!

00:14:16.010 --> 00:14:24.390
